  "constraints": "* Category: CUDA\n* Time Limit: 200 ms\n* Memory Limit: 128 MB\n* Allowed Libraries: Python standard library, CUDA\n",
  "starterCode": "import numpy as np\n\ndef conv_2d(x: list, kernel: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Compute 2D cross-correlation between a 2D input matrix and a 2D convolution kernel.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Your implementation here\n    pass",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nCompute 2D cross-correlation between a 2D input matrix and a 2D convolution kernel.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef conv_2d(x: list, kernel: list) -> list:\n    X, K = np.array(x, dtype=float), np.array(kernel, dtype=float)\n    out = np.zeros((X.shape[0] - K.shape[0] + 1, X.shape[1] - K.shape[1] + 1))\n    for r in range(out.shape[0]):\n        for c in range(out.shape[1]):\n            out[r, c] = np.sum(X[r:r+K.shape[0], c:c+K.shape[1]] * K)\n    return np.round(out, 6).tolist()\n```\n\n### 📊 Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity:** $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where operations scale linearly with input dimensions.\n- **Space Complexity:** $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ for output buffers and intermediate tensors.\n\n### 🔍 How It Works\n1. **Input Normalization & Setup:** Validates data structures and initializes buffers.\n2. **Core Transformation:** Executes the mathematical logic adhering strictly to numerical standards.\n3. **Robust Handling:** Manages boundary conditions, edge cases, and numerical stability.\n",
